  "summary": "A visual style where colors are forced to match limited 8-bit color palettes, creating distinctive color mapping.",
  "description": "It appears as abrupt color transitions where full-color images are reduced to 256 colors, often with dithering to smooth transitions. This emerged from technical limitations of early computer and console graphics systems, particularly the 8-bit era, and has been revived as a retro aesthetic in modern digital art.",
  "culturalContext": "8-bit palette snapping — the constraint of 256 (or fewer) colors forcing gradients into visible steps — is the defining look of early computing and modern retro pixel art: NES's 25-color reality, EGA/VGA dithering patterns, web-safe 216 colors, and games (Hyper Light Drifter, Shovel Knight) that design within snaps. The aesthetic makes banding and dither pattern aesthetic subjects; \"palette swap\" culture turns the constraint into a design sport.",
